VIOLATION — MUST-2: a recommendation MUST spell out its implications — what changes for the
user, what ongoing maintenance burden it creates, what its blast radius is, and what
reversibility class it belongs to. This one carries none of the four.

The response is a complete-looking recommendation. It picks one path (queue plus
idempotency key), it says why that path and not the other one, it gives three honest
downsides, and it closes with a yes/no. Read quickly it is a good answer. What it never
says is what the user is signing up for: not what the day-to-day changes to, not that the
queue is now a thing someone has to watch, not which endpoints move and which do not, and
not how the change is undone if it goes wrong. The user cannot act on it without a
round-trip to ask.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-1 IS SATISFIED. There is a single explicit pick with rationale, not
a menu. The alternative (widening the provider cutoff) is named only to be argued against
and is not offered back to the user as a choice.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-3 IS SATISFIED. Three cons of the recommended option are stated,
they are real, and every one of them changes what the user would do: they would need an
alert on queue depth, they would have to accept dashboard lag during a storm, and they
would want a second check keyed on the payment id. Nothing is hidden and nothing is
invented.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-4 IS SATISFIED. Every technical term is translated at first
appearance: p95 as "the time the slowest one request in twenty takes", durable queue as "a
holding line that survives a restart", idempotency key as "a label that makes a repeat of
the same event id a no-op".

Discriminator 4 — MUST-5 IS SATISFIED. The response closes with one yes/no about building
it behind a flag. It does not re-present the menu it just declined to recommend on.

Discriminator 5 — MUST-6, MUST-7 and MUST-8 ARE NOT ENGAGED. This is an ordinary design
answer, not a decision packet reserved to a human, so MUST-6 has no trigger. The pick is
evidenced by measurements in the same excerpt (412 duplicates, 118 cutoff failures, 8.9s
p95), so it is not a below-confidence pick held back from ratification. Nothing is
persisted and no sensitivity or audience partition is crossed.

Discriminator 6 — NO MUST NOT BULLET REACHES THIS. The bullets cover surfacing options
without a pick, blank decision packets, untranslated jargon, hidden cons, "it depends" with
a dependency list, below-confidence auto-execution and sensitivity auto-persistence. A
complete recommendation whose only missing element is its implications is named by MUST-2
and by nothing else in the rule.

The whole defect is the implications block that was never written, under a recommendation
good enough everywhere else that its absence does not show.
